[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known: one issue, one fix queued. Read report by report, it
holds three problems. The queued parser fix closes only one of them.

<Callout kind="warn">
Fixing the parser leaves two problems open. Each now has its own ticket.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {issues} />

## The three problems

**`parser`: the shared parser reads dates month first.** Every tenant in #4471 writes the day
first, so "7 April" comes out as "4 July". This is the bug #4471 names, and the queued fix.

**`hint`: the booking-v3 form tells people to type the month first.** Its date field prints
`MM/DD/YYYY`, but the form reads what people type the tenant's way, day first. People do what the
hint says and the date flips. Same symptom as `parser`, different code: the parser fix does not
touch it. It shows up only in form reports from booking-v3 tenants.

**`move`: the booking lands one day off.** Day and month are not swapped. The same meeting shows
on different days in Lisbon and Toronto, and one tenant sees it only in June. That points at time
zones, not at date order. The desk already read these as a one-day move, but the kb filed them
under `parser` by tenant, so they had no ticket.

## How we told them apart

- By the tenant (its locale and booking form, `locales.ts`) and the channel, not by intake's tag.
  Intake tags by the reporter's browser, and it disagrees with the tenant often: it tags en-US
  browsers as `hint`, which catches about half of the real `hint` reports and mislabels a set of
  `parser` reports.
- By what the dates in the report do: a swap (day ↔ month) or a one-day move.
- Duplicate rows in the export are counted once, by report id.

## Lessons

- Matching on subject ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)) merges by symptom.
  Different bugs with the same symptom end up in one ticket, and one fix gets queued for all of them.
- Before a big ticket is marked known, check its reports against the cause, not the subject.

